id: k8s.etcd
kind: kubernetes
title: etcd
aliases: etcd, cluster datastore
tags: architecture, control-plane, state
questions:
  - what happens if etcd is lost?
invariant: etcd is the only stateful store; it holds every object and is a quorum-replicated log, so writes need a majority and the cluster needs a working quorum.
answer_30s: etcd holds the entire cluster state as a quorum-replicated key/value log: lose quorum and the control plane goes read-only; lose the data and the cluster state is gone.
answer_2m: |
  Quorum is the part to reason about: three members tolerate one failure, five
  tolerate two, and an even number adds no tolerance. Write latency in etcd is
  the throughput ceiling of the whole control plane (fsync bound), which is why
  chatty controllers are an architectural problem, not just a CPU problem. It
  stores desired and observed state, not application data, and not container
  filesystems. Operationally: backups plus a tested restore, TLS between
  members, a separate disk, and alerting on member health and leader elections.
when_to_use:
  - capacity, backup/restore and control-plane availability reasoning
commands:
  - kubectl get pods -n kube-system => is the etcd member set healthy
  - kubectl -n kube-system logs <etcd-pod> => slow-apply and leader-election warnings
failure_modes:
  - quorum loss from an even member count losing two members
  - no restore rehearsal, so backups are unproven
  - disk contention that turns etcd latency into cluster-wide API timeouts
follow_ups:
  - how do you restore a cluster from an etcd snapshot?
related:
  - k8s.api-server
  - k8s.controller
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/
